  "ablation_size_label": "scored case: 4096x5120x4096 (ranking_by LAST)",
  "takeaways": [
    "**The check is bit-exact, so only tensor cores qualify:** `eval.py` re-checks every benchmark input at rtol 1e-5, and with fp16 outputs near 1024 that is an exact match against cuBLAS. A probe (no Crisp) found `mma.sync` m16n8k16 with f32 accumulation summed in K order matches cuBLAS on **all 21M outputs** on H100, A100 and L4, while an IEEE fp32 FMA loop misses 3.7-7% by one ulp. Every Crisp kernel here is a tensor-core kernel with no split-K -- including the small-shape fallback.",
    "**The field is cuBLAS:** #1 / median on 2026-10-27 were H100 205 / 221 us, B200 92 / 114, A100 596 / 683, L4 1990 / 2255 -- most entries call `torch.matmul`. Crisp entries rank H100 41/43, A100 59/60, L4 25/25.",
    "**H100 -- the kernel is the benchmark suite's, the transpose was the surprise:** wgmma reads B K-major and the contest's `b` is (K N) N-contiguous. A per-arm diagnostic on the scored case: the 2wg_deep kernel 306-309 us (~560 TFLOPS, matching the suite's 545 at 4096^3), torch's `b.t()` copy **160 us**. A tiled Crisp transpose (`transpose_b.crisp`, coalesced both sides through a 64x65 shared tile) takes **39 us**: 462 -> 340 us. wgmma transB (on `plan/scramble.md`) would remove it.",
    "**TMA multicast does not help 2wg_deep:** a 2x2-cluster multicast variant was correct and slower (366 vs 309 us); the two-warpgroup tile already reuses each TMA load twice.",
    "**A100 / L4 needed compiler work (endeavour 196):** the Ampere `cp.async` load-tile refused 16-bit tiles (cp.async moves 4/8/16 bytes) -- it now copies 16-byte chunks spread over every thread; the register fit-check refused a 4-warp distributed tile; and `await` on a cp.async barrier lacked the CTA barrier after `wait_group` (2.8% wrong outputs once tiles were shared across warps). A100 6.78 -> 4.96 ms. The remaining gap is operand fragments read from shared memory one 2-byte element at a time (no `ldmatrix`).",
    "**Packaging:** a TMA kernel takes 65 parameters, more than an inline `asm` statement accepts, so this entry loads the PTX through the CUDA driver API (`dlopen` libcuda, `cuModuleLoadDataEx`) with the launch ABI read out of the CUDA hoist. `CU_JIT_THREADS_PER_BLOCK` stands in for `__launch_bounds__`; without it the 288-thread wgmma block failed to launch.",
    "**B200 -- tcgen05, then the epilogue:** the B200 path is Crisp's tcgen05 kernel (endeavours 208/209: accumulator in tensor memory, warp-specialized producer / MMA / epilogue). v6 transposed b first (265 us); tcgen05 `:b-major :mn` (endeavour 213) read it as given (230 us). Pricing v7 one piece at a time on popcorn (no rental) found the real cost: with the epilogue's stores removed it ran 147.5 us -- the tcgen05 layout hands each thread a whole row, and it left as 4-byte stores, 32 rows per warp instruction. 16-byte stores (endeavour 215) plus a 128x128 tile for the scored shape's 2.16-wave quantization: **156 us**, rank 41/53. Measured and rejected on the way: persistent scheduling (~3%), and a 2-SM `cta_group::2` pair (exact on metal, +7-9% only on full-wave shapes; endeavour 214, parked).",
    "**ldmatrix closed a third of the A100 gap (endeavour 216):** a twin A/B on one A100 (same compiler, ldmatrix switched off) took the scored shape from 4204 to 3032 us, bit-exact; the 3-slot cp.async ring adds ~5%. What is left (A100 ~2.9 ms vs cuBLAS ~0.64) is, by arithmetic, shared-memory bank conflicts: the scratch tiles' 64 B / 256 B row pitches make the ldmatrix reads 4- and 8-way conflicted. A padded or swizzled scratch layout is the next lever (tests/spec/216-ldmatrix/ldmatrix.md, D-216-5)."
  ],
  "beauty_entry": {
    "crisp_file": "06-matmul-v2/matmul_beauty.crisp",
